        "Lack of standardized battery designs across OEMs complicating disassembly and recycling processes, increasing costs by 30-40%",
        "Geopolitical risks in sourcing recycled materials from conflict zones (e.g., Democratic Republic of Congo for cobalt)",
        "Skill gaps in the workforce, with only 5% of recycling technicians trained in advanced battery dismantling techniques"
    ],

    "future_outlook": "By 2030, India's battery recycling sector is expected to achieve a recycling rate of 50-60% for lithium-ion batteries, up from <10% in 2023, driven by mandatory EPR targets and technological advancements. The market will shift from informal to formal dominance, with 70% of recycling conducted by registered entities. Hydrometallurgical methods will become the dominant technology, accounting for 60% of capacity, while pyrometallurgical and direct recycling methods will cater to niche applications. The sector will witness $10-15 billion in cumulative investments by 2035, supported by PLI schemes and carbon credit incentives. Key growth regions will include Gujarat (for lithium-ion recycling), Tamil Nadu (for lead-acid), and Karnataka (for electronics). Global partnerships, particularly with the EU and US, will accelerate technology transfer in battery recycling. However, achieving 70% recycling efficiency by 2030 will require overcoming policy enforcement challenges, reducing CAPEX by 40% through modular designs, and training 100,000+ technicians in advanced recycling techniques. The sector's success will be a critical enabler for India's EV adoption targets of 30% by 2030 and 80% by 2047.",

